<title>SWITZERLAND: ABB H1 NET PROFIT RISES 2.0 PCT.</title>
<headline>ABB H1 NET PROFIT RISES 2.0 PCT.</headline>
<dateline>ZURICH 1997-08-07</dateline>
<text>
<p>Six months to June 30, 1997</p>
<p>				     (million dollars unless stated)</p>
<p> Group net profit	  567		    vs 556</p>
<p> Operating earnings</p>
<p> after depreciation	1,086		  vs 1,050</p>
<p> Group pre-tax profit    943		    vs 940</p>
<p> Minority interest	 9			vs 6</p>
<p> Taxes			 367		    vs 378</p>
<p> Revenue		     15,198		 vs 15,608</p>
<p> Materials expenses	6,733		  vs 7,348</p>
<p> Personnel expenses	4,863		  vs 5,143</p>
<p> Other expenses	    2,409		  vs 2,567</p>
<p> Depreciation		492		    vs 513</p>
<p> Unusual items	     16		     vs 10</p>
<p> Interest income	   164		    vs 186</p>
<p> Interest expense	  315		    vs 307</p>
<p>NOTE - Full name is ABB Asea Brown Boveri AG. On average analysts had forecast a group net profit of $581 million. The forecasts ranged from $511 million to $600 million.</p>
<p>-- Peter Nielsen, Zurich Editorial +41 1 631 7340</p>
